  <body>
    <main>
      <header>
        <h1>{title}</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          This page is a practical reference for choosing a knitting machine—without getting buried in brand wars or
          “best machine” lists. You’ll leave with a clear short-list and the right questions to ask (especially if you’re
          buying used).
        </p>

        <div class="callout">
          <strong>Fast answer:</strong> Choose based on <strong>what you want to knit</strong> and <strong>the yarn weight you’ll use most</strong>.
          That points you to the right <strong>gauge</strong>. After that, confirm the machine is <strong>complete</strong> and <strong>working</strong>.
        </div>
      </header>

      <div class="grid">
        <div>
          <section aria-labelledby="start">
            <h2 id="start">Start here: what do you want to knit?</h2>
            <p class="mini">
              Think in “most often,” not “someday.” Hats and cozy accessories want a different setup than fine baby items or lace.
              Your best machine is the one that matches your real-life projects and yarn.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section aria-labelledby="gauge">
            <h2 id="gauge">Step 1: Match your yarn to the machine gauge</h2>
            <p class="mini">
              Machine gauge is needle spacing, and it determines what yarn weights you can knit comfortably.
            </p>

            <div class="card">
              <h3>Quick guide</h3>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>Bulky / Chunky</strong> — for bulkier yarns (including heavy worsted)</li>
                <li><strong>Mid-gauge</strong> — for sport weight to light worsted yarns</li>
                <li><strong>Standard gauge</strong> — for sock/fingering/baby weight yarns</li>
                <li><strong>Fine gauge</strong> — for lace weight to fingering weight yarns</li>
              </ul>
              <p class="mini">
                You don’t need special coned yarn to start. Cones are popular because they reduce yarn ends, but regular yarn works too.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section aria-labelledby="bedtype">
            <h2 id="bedtype">Step 2: Plastic bed vs metal bed</h2>
            <p class="mini">This is the “feel” decision. Both can be great—what matters is matching your expectations.</p>

            <div class="card">
              <ul>
                <li>
                  <strong>Plastic bed machines</strong> — often a lower-cost starting point; commonly used with heavier yarns; fewer accessories.
                </li>
                <li>
                  <strong>Metal bed machines</strong> — typically more features and accessory options (including patterning on many models);
                  more gauge choices across machine families.
                </li>
              </ul>
              <p class="mini">
                Many knitters start plastic bed and later move to metal bed when they want to expand what they can do.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section aria-labelledby="used">
            <h2 id="used">Step 3: If you’re buying used, ask these questions</h2>
            <p class="mini">
              Used machines can be a fantastic value—if the machine is complete and demonstrably works.
            </p>

            <div class="card">
              <ol class="checklist">
                <li>
                  <strong>What brand and model is it?</strong>
                  <div class="mini">Many used machines are Japanese brands; Passap machines are a different style of machine.</div>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <strong>What gauge is it?</strong>
                  <div class="mini">If the seller doesn’t know, ask them to measure needle spacing in millimeters (center-to-center).</div>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <strong>How old is it—and has it been cared for?</strong>
                  <div class="mini">Ask about storage, any rust, and whether it was used heavily (production knitting vs home use).</div>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <strong>What’s included?</strong>
                  <div class="mini">
                    At minimum: needle bed with needles, carriage (often 2 pieces), tension mast, instruction manual, hand tools,
                    and power cord if electronic.
                  </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <strong>Is it working?</strong>
                  <div class="mini">Ask for a short demonstration or photos/video of it knitting with the exact parts being sold.</div>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <strong>Is the instruction manual included?</strong>
                  <div class="mini">You can often find manuals online, but having the correct one makes setup far easier.</div>
                </li>
              </ol>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section aria-labelledby="next">
            <h2 id="next">What to do when you’ve found a “maybe” machine</h2>
            <div class="card">
              <p class="mini"><strong>Send yourself (or the seller) this mini checklist:</strong></p>
              <ul>
                <li>Brand + model number</li>
                <li>Gauge (or needle spacing in mm)</li>
                <li>What’s included (list it)</li>
                <li>Proof it knits (video/photo/demo)</li>
                <li>Where it was stored + any rust/damage</li>
                <li>Your main project type + your most-used yarn weight</li>
              </ul>

              <p class="mini">
                Want a simple “yes/no” gut-check? If you can’t confirm the gauge and it can’t be demonstrated knitting, pass and keep looking.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <hr />

          <section aria-labelledby="faq">
            <h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>

            <details open>
              <summary>What should I decide first before buying a knitting machine?</summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                Start with what you want to knit most often and the yarn weight you want to use most. That determines the machine gauge you should look for.
              </div>
            </details>

            <details>
              <summary>What does knitting machine gauge mean?</summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                Gauge describes needle spacing and what yarn weights the machine can handle. Common categories include bulky/chunky, mid-gauge, standard gauge, and fine gauge.
              </div>
            </details>

            <details>
              <summary>Do I need special coned yarn?</summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                No. Cones are popular because they reduce yarn ends, but you can use regular yarn too.
              </div>
            </details>

            <details>
              <summary>Plastic bed or metal bed—what’s better?</summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                Plastic bed machines are often a lower-cost starting point and commonly use heavier yarns. Metal bed machines typically offer more features and accessory options (including patterning on many models) and more gauge choices.
              </div>
            </details>

            <details>
              <summary>How do I figure out the gauge if the seller doesn’t know?</summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                Ask the seller to measure (in millimeters) the distance from the center of one needle to the center of the next needle.
              </div>
            </details>

            <details>
              <summary>What must be included when buying a used machine?</summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                At minimum: needle bed with needles, carriage (often 2 pieces), tension mast, instruction manual, hand tools, and power cord if electronic.
              </div>
            </details>

            <details>
              <summary>Are used machines a good idea?</summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                Yes—used machines can be a great value if they’re complete and in working order. Confirm what’s included, verify it knits, and watch for rust or storage damage.
              </div>
            </details>

            <details>
              <summary>Is machine knitting cheating?</summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                No. It’s a tool that speeds up repetitive knitting—similar to using a sewing machine in sewing.
              </div>
            </details>
          </section>

          <hr />

          <section aria-labelledby="contact">
            <h2 id="contact">Still deciding?</h2>
            <p class="mini">
              If you want a quick second set of eyes, send the model number(s), the gauge (or needle spacing in mm), what’s included, and what you want to knit.
            </p>
            <!-- Replace this link with your preferred next step -->
            <p><a href="/contact">Contact</a></p>
          </section>
        </div>

        <aside>
          <div class="card">
            <h3>One-minute decision helper</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Yarn weight first</strong> → choose the gauge</li>
              <li><strong>Used?</strong> → confirm it’s complete</li>
              <li><strong>Used?</strong> → confirm it knits</li>
              <li><strong>Then</strong> compare price, condition, extras</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="card" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
            <h3>Good to know</h3>
            <p class="mini">
              Many beginners get overwhelmed by buttons and levers. The fastest path is consistent practice, avoiding too many add-ons too soon,
              and choosing one method for patterns (software, charting by hand, or a charting device).
            </p>

            <h2>Prefer to buy a new machine?</h2>
            <div class="card">
              <p class="mini">
                Some knitters prefer starting with a <strong>new</strong> machine for simplicity, current documentation,
                and a straightforward buying process.
              </p>

              <p class="mini">
                <strong>For now:</strong> new knitting machines are available through Knit It Now.
              </p>

              <p>
                <a href="https://www.knititnow.com/path/4045/knitting-machines-for-sale">
                  View new knitting machines for sale
                </a>
              </p>
          </div>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
